      "id": "mobile-driving-licence-mdl-based-on-iso-iec-18013-5-b06ubj",
      "sector": "mobility",
      "title": "Mobile Driving Licence (mDL) based on ISO/IEC 18013-5",
      "summary": "The mobile driving licence (mDL) is a digital version of a physical licence, held in a secure wallet on a smartphone. Built on ISO/IEC 18013-5, it enables trusted, privacy-preserving verification of licence data in person and, via ISO/IEC 18013-7, online. Selective disclosure lets holders share only requested data, while signatures, device binding, and holder authentication ensure the data is authentic and unaltered.\n\nSince 2014, experts have collaborated through 100+ ISO meetings on a generic, interoperable model for verifiable credentials, initially focused on mDLs. In 2018, AAMVA, eReg, and Austroads formalised cooperation in their first global summit. Stakeholders collaborate further in test events (15 held). ISO/IEC 18013-5 was published in 2021, and Part 7 in 2024. The mdoc model supports many credential types, with growing adoption of Photo IDs.\n\nThis work has driven policy adoption, including the US REAL ID Modernization Act, the DHS mDL Rule, implementing acts for the EUDI Wallet, and more. Millions of ISO-compliant mDLs are provisioned across 23 AAMVA jurisdictions, next to mdoc deployments in Japan, Australia, New Zealand, Philippines, India, Argentina and the EU.",
