      "userJourney": "ISO/IEC 18013-5 defines three roles: an Issuer, a Holder's Wallet on a mobile device, and a Reader run by a Relying Party. The Issuer provisions the mDL into the Wallet using a standardised doctype and namespaces. To present it, the Holder opens the Wallet and connects to the Reader via QR code or NFC tap, then exchanges data over Bluetooth, Wi-Fi Aware or NFC. The Reader requests data elements; the Holder consents, and the Wallet returns only approved data, signed and bound to the device. This enables secure, selective, privacy-preserving offline verification of transaction data. ISO/IEC 18013-7 adds a remote, online mechanism. Since the mdoc model is generic, the same architecture extends to other credentials, such as Photo IDs.\n\nInteroperability test events were at the core of collaboration to internationally recognise mDLs. Since 2018, AAMVA, eReg and Austroads have endorsed test events that brought issuers, wallets and readers together, pairing real implementations from different countries and vendors device to device to run engagement, data exchange and verification live, identifying gaps and maturing the global mDL ecosystem.",
